{"name":"Wind Cave","hex":"#686c7b","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Wind Cave","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A breathing cave in western South Dakota, United States, designated a national park and notable for its boxwork."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Wind_Cave"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wind Cave National Park","blocks":[{"text":"Wind Cave National Park is a national park of the United States located 10 miles (16 km) north of the town of Hot Springs in western South Dakota. Established on January 3, 1903 by President Theodore Roosevelt, it was the sixth national park in the U.S. and the first cave to be designated a national park anywhere in the world. The cave has calcite formations known as boxwork, as well as frostwork. Approximately 95 percent of the world's discovered boxwork formations are found in Wind Cave."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Skyway_Lake%2C_stalactites%2C_Wind_Cave.jpg/330px-Skyway_Lake%2C_stalactites%2C_Wind_Cave.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind_Cave_National_Park","wikidata":"Q1334313"},{"title":"Wind","blocks":[{"text":"Wind is the natural movement of air or other gases relative to a planet's surface. Winds occur on a range of scales, from thunderstorm flows lasting tens of minutes, to local breezes generated by heating of land surfaces and lasting a few hours, to global winds resulting from the difference in absorption of solar energy between the climate zones on Earth. The study of wind is known as anemology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif/330px-Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind","wikidata":"Q8094"},{"title":"Cave","blocks":[{"text":"Caves and caverns are natural voids under the surface of the Earth and have also been observed in other rocky worlds. Caves often form by the weathering of rock and can extend deep underground. Exogene caves are smaller openings that extend a relatively short distance underground. Caves which extend farther underground than the opening is wide are called endogene caves."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Lechuguilla_Cave_Pearlsian_Gulf.jpg/330px-Lechuguilla_Cave_Pearlsian_Gulf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cave","wikidata":"Q35509"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Black_Hills_from_Harney_Peak.jpg/500px-Black_Hills_from_Harney_Peak.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Black_Hills_from_Harney_Peak.jpg/500px-Black_Hills_from_Harney_Peak.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlack_Hills_from_Harney_Peak.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Badlands_NP_2006-09-03_%28241257945%29.jpg/500px-Badlands_NP_2006-09-03_%28241257945%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Badlands_NP_2006-09-03_%28241257945%29.jpg/500px-Badlands_NP_2006-09-03_%28241257945%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABadlands_NP_2006-09-03_(241257945).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/A556%2C_Wind_Cave_National_Park%2C_South_Dakota%2C_USA%2C_2001.jpg/500px-A556%2C_Wind_Cave_National_Park%2C_South_Dakota%2C_USA%2C_2001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimed